Welcome to the world of SIGEL. You want to use it? It's not as difficult
as baking pancake. SIGEL runs on Linux.

1. Building SIGEL

To build SIGEL you need g++ with C++20, Qt 6, OpenGL with GLU, and libtirpc.
In the top directory of the repository, first unpack the supporting libraries
once:

mkdir -p downloads
tar xzf vendor/supportingLibs.tar.gz -C downloads
tar xzf vendor/pvm3.4.6.tgz -C downloads/supportingLibs --strip-components=1 ./pvm3

Then run

make

This builds the supporting libraries, PVM and the three executables. Then it fills
the directory "sigelApp", from which SIGEL is started, with:
- the executables "sigel", "sigel_slave" and "manage_dyn_slave" (for dynamic clients, see below)
- the scripts "sigelLauncher" and "sigelDynClient" (for dynamic clients, see below)
- the file "stdConf.mt"
- the subdirectories pixmaps and textures
- the subdirectory supportingLibs/pvm3, a link to the PVM that was built

To build and run the unit tests in the directory "sigel/tests", which use Qt Test, run

make test

2. Getting SIGEL to work

Start SIGEL in the directory "sigelApp" with the script "sigelLauncher":

./sigelLauncher                         starts SIGEL with its windows
./sigelLauncher -evolve <experiment>    evolves the experiment without windows until its
                                        termination condition is reached, then saves it
                                        to the same file
./sigelLauncher -devolve <experiment>   the same, and dynamic clients can join (see 2.6)

On one machine nothing else is needed. Sections 2.1 and 2.2 are for an evolution on
several hosts.

All SIGELs of one user share one PVM daemon, and each SIGEL stops it when it ends.
So if you close SIGEL's windows while an evolution without windows runs, that
evolution stops too. To look at results during such a run, give the windows a
daemon of their own:

PVM_VMID=view ./sigelLauncher

2.1 Evolving on several hosts

The master is the host on which you start SIGEL. Through PVM it starts the program
"sigel_slave" on the other hosts.

The experiment exists on the master only. Do not copy it to the other hosts. The master
sends the data of the experiment to each slave, and each slave sends one fitness value
back.

On each other host:
- Build SIGEL as in section 1. The directory "sigelApp" must be writable.
- Set two environment variables in the start-up file of the shell:

  SIGEL_ROOT    the full path of the directory "sigelApp"
  PVM_ROOT      $SIGEL_ROOT/supportingLibs/pvm3

  With bash this file is ".bashrc". On Debian and Ubuntu put the two lines at the top of
  the file, above the lines that end it for a shell that is not interactive.

The master must reach each other host by its host name. A host behind NAT cannot take
part.

2.2 The remote shell

The master starts PVM on the other hosts through a remote shell. On the master:

- Set up ssh so that "ssh <host>" logs in to each other host with a key and without a
  password. Log in once by hand, so that ssh knows the key of the host.
- If your login name on the other host is different, give it in "~/.ssh/config":

  Host <host>
      User <login name>

- "sigelLauncher" tells PVM to use ssh: it sets PVM_RSH to "/usr/bin/ssh". To use a
  different remote shell, set PVM_RSH before you start "sigelLauncher":

  export PVM_RSH=<path of the remote shell>

- Check the variables of the other host. This must print both paths:

  ssh <host> 'echo $SIGEL_ROOT $PVM_ROOT'

- Check the address that "sigelLauncher" prints at its start. Debian and Ubuntu map the
  name of the master to 127.0.1.1, which the other hosts cannot reach. In that case
  "sigelLauncher" gives PVM the address of the default route in a host file and prints
  it, so "/etc/hosts" needs no change. If the other hosts are on a different network,
  for example behind a second network card or with a VPN on, give PVM the right address
  of the master in a host file of your own:

  echo "<host name of the master> ip=<network address of the master>" > ~/pvmhosts
  export PVMHOSTFILE=~/pvmhosts

2.3 The sigelLauncher script

The script sets the environment variables that SIGEL needs on the master and starts
"sigel" with the arguments it was given. If the name of the master maps to 127.0.1.1,
it also writes the PVM host file of section 2.2 and removes it when "sigel" ends. It
takes the directory it is started in as SIGEL_ROOT. To start it from another directory,
for example from cron, set SIGEL_ROOT in the script to the full path of "sigelApp". A
crontab entry can then be:

00      21      20      8       *       /home/user/sigelApp/sigelLauncher -evolve insect.exp

2.4 Using gnuplot

SIGEL can plot the fitness curves with gnuplot. For this, gnuplot must be in a directory
of your PATH variable.

2.5 Rendering the POV-Ray export

With the format POV, the movie settings write one scene file per frame, an include file,
the frame list <prefix>.pov and <prefix>.ini. In the output directory, "povray <prefix>.ini"
renders every frame at the output size. Povray 3.7 must be in a directory of your PATH variable.

2.6 Dynamic clients and sigelDynClient

A dynamic client is a host that joins an evolution while it runs. Prepare it as in 2.1
and 2.2. It needs no experiment file.

On the master:

./sigelLauncher -devolve <experiment>

The master prints "Server is awaiting requests from dynamic clients on port 6789.."
and starts the evolution. From then on a client can join. On the client, in the
directory "sigelApp":

./sigelDynClient <host name of the master> [<number of slaves>]

The script copies "sigel_slave" to "/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP" and gives the host name
of the client and the number of slaves to the master. The master must reach the client
by this name; it is the name that "hostname" prints on the client. The master then
runs up to that number of slaves on the client at the same time. Without a number,
it is the number of processors of the client.

The master and the client must have the same version of SIGEL.

For each fitness value the master prints the individual and the host that sent it:

	Fitness <value> for individual "<name>" from host "<host>".

A dynamic client stays until the master ends. The script then removes the directory
"/tmp/_SIGEL_EVOLUTION_TEMP" and ends. The PVM daemon on the client ends when the
master ends.
